e {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fcbb935c-0e69-53bf-8029-fbd172319da8" :question/math-academy-id "q-104036" :question/problem "Select the function that has a discontinuity at $x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is shown below. Notice that the function contains a jump exactly at $x=-2$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n![](images/05/05ad378968e143e83dab8d50608e6db7ccaf97f5e1a8f2ee83d2f893e3d820c3.png)\n\nAll the other given graphs can be plotted without taking the pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8bd6dd-1931-5df3-803b-dc0e5167142e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238463} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8ed7c5-b2af-580b-8113-c798acb35065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238721} {:answer/id #uuid "92b58f29-e9d2-52ea-80a1-a85417e0693d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239321} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b9cbc0-4187-50a5-81fd-c6539f6fe9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239932} {:answer/id #uuid "f9aeed86-3876-53b5-b2e9-7b048f60598b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9aeed86-3876-53b5-b2e9-7b048f60598b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240264} :answer-field/id #uuid "42c90896-bfe8-504a-9692-0915b93a4eb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc9393f2-809c-5769-b329-186102da51c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-104051" :question/problem "![](images/65/65b9b32e8fac6a71141ce820855058367e9235129de7334db35a200905474cc0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=5$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(5)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a jump exactly at $x=5$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129102 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd6703d2-36f6-5665-90e3-f75aea5f663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Domain of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157a8223-0c9b-5529-8733-655ea9476c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238233} {:answer/id #uuid "2412dd69-dc1d-5afb-b423-8cac607f549c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238372} {:answer/id #uuid "b0aa80ef-c925-5891-9d12-dd7bc86a59a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239590} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ebce3a-e306-509f-97b8-f30222fa0ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239830} {:answer/id #uuid "f47fc246-ebae-5589-8afa-6aa1ff9c0ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2412dd69-dc1d-5afb-b423-8cac607f549c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238372} :answer-field/id #uuid "601a065b-da49-5c92-b360-c74641979347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "33ee86bf-83f2-58e4-8a42-08be20a94a04" :question/math-academy-id "q-10406" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092385 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5ae20c-b789-5249-bff8-1194daf3c008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238321} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc902fd-c7c3-53b3-90a3-02661fb40990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239711} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c92a83-db3b-57d5-87ad-2844ddc86d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239896} {:answer/id #uuid "ee996a51-be22-5564-9782-5ded740f8f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240158} {:answer/id #uuid "f14cea86-7dde-5527-ac9c-2767c9873574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdc902fd-c7c3-53b3-90a3-02661fb40990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239711} :answer-field/id #uuid "092d888b-3d25-51dd-8dad-08178716d6c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9348de04-831f-5ba3-963b-71f2ffb24276" :question/math-academy-id "q-104073" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}$ at the point $(8,12)$ intersects the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(8)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{8}}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-12 & =-(x-8) \\\\ y & =-x+8+12 \\\\ y & =20-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =20-0 \\\\ y & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,20).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50ff7ec6-76c4-5b74-bf1d-be84c817c24b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238755} {:answer/id #uuid "54122348-0530-5cb3-b939-847df168d38e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(3)$  does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238777} {:answer/id #uuid "a33bcdd9-91d5-5f8b-a44e-d43311963d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239476} {:answer/id #uuid "c0cdaeed-395b-51a0-82a0-64a886e4efb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239737} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1fa30c-32a7-5587-8fe4-d9afe2475fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54122348-0530-5cb3-b939-847df168d38e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(3)$  does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238777} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb6443c8-739f-5411-886c-c1f2567fc824" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb39799c-0b6e-5be1-b620-9cc6473bd49a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104129" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=3?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{x-3}, & x<3 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{x-3}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- We have that $f(3)$ is not defined since $x=3$ does not belong to the domain of $f(x).$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$ because $f(3)$ does not exist."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11b040a2-7645-58f8-9b41-03e61af92932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238203} {:answer/id #uuid "c63d1d7b-fe91-5f68-8507-be316b42d719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239799} {:answer/id #uuid "d99a8202-4c5f-58ae-b994-4605c367f59b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-2)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239952} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6d06d9-c507-5f74-a51e-07af771eddee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240118} {:answer/id #uuid "eab4ba20-4e19-5aba-a102-ad9128d50961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eab4ba20-4e19-5aba-a102-ad9128d50961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240120} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca346b04-01ff-54ea-ad48-0975c9bdfa88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65ba7f63-40be-5e71-8f5e-9f52e8de1734" :question/math-academy-id "q-104132" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-2?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2}{2}, & x<-2 \\\\ 5, & x=-2 \\\\ \\frac{18}{{x}^{2}+2}, & x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- $f(-2)=5,$ so $f(-2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+2}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)^{2}+2}{2} \\\\  & =3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(\\frac{18}{{x}^{2}+2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{(-2)^{2}+2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=3.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=3,$ while $f(-2)=5.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f089e05-0b92-5d54-8257-ef620a1c2e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)\\ne f(-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238317} {:answer/id #uuid "2c117c1e-b599-54be-96b1-26023faa5f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238447} {:answer/id #uuid "509a042c-0027-580b-8096-62ace31e1b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238752} {:answer/id #uuid "bba9e6d4-ebc8-5a12-9275-5a3db937a009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-\\sqrt{3})$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239690} {:answer/id #uuid "f08fffcd-7647-5b3e-99f0-bc5415338449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f089e05-0b92-5d54-8257-ef620a1c2e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)\\ne f(-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238317} :answer-field/id #uuid "8734b4ce-4096-5667-a80d-acd673367dd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2dc1316-63b7-56de-ae30-4568e3d25064" :question/math-academy-id "q-104148" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-\\sqrt{3}?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{3}, & x<-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3, & x=-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+3}, & x>-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- $f(-\\sqrt{3})=3,$ so $f(-\\sqrt{3})$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+3}{3} \\\\  & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)=2.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)=2,$ while $f(-\\sqrt{3})=3.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)\\ne f(-\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059678 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6afb4058-908a-5983-9c1e-78ee3fbadf44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives Using Leibnitz Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa44a27-ef68-5389-9758-f884a5d93477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238277} {:answer/id #uuid "2415dd70-94cd-5924-9d0e-22cf05a020c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}+cos⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238374} {:answer/id #uuid "93cb1ee1-225c-5deb-b3e5-a0bf21607a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}-sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239332} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1e2a22-2c42-5e28-96bc-5600c10b0095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}+2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239420} {:answer/id #uuid "b15c3867-b8c6-5277-b1af-d18fc8689a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}-2cos⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aa44a27-ef68-5389-9758-f884a5d93477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238277} :answer-field/id #uuid "3465f331-8843-5ef7-b5b2-36db197078df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7aef485-7834-5512-aabf-9615a3c07b9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104192" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+2sin⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+2sin⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-1/2}+2sin⁡x) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-1/2-1}+2cos⁡x \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-3/2}+2cos⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{-3/2}+2cos⁡x) \\\\  & =-2(-\\frac{3}{2}){x}^{-3/2-1}-2sin⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-2sin⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096617 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20897b11-31f3-5236-8344-066364ab30aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}cos⁡x-{e}^{x}sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238339} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9dc0f2-2211-5b4e-a2e7-26a81ec7f21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}cos⁡x+sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238465} {:answer/id #uuid "528c811e-a747-5b55-b5c7-a2d707220097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}cos⁡x-{e}^{x}sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238765} {:answer/id #uuid "c9cd3281-ae65-5560-8442-2a603e50ff12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}cos⁡x+sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239827} {:answer/id #uuid "cce47b31-03c3-57e4-ae08-02ac34ba2a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}cos⁡x+{e}^{x}sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "528c811e-a747-5b55-b5c7-a2d707220097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}cos⁡x-{e}^{x}sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238765} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe0d6216-93d3-5d7b-aefb-cbdf2e8a0a46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d7240cf-a773-5ae8-8c7e-03955317789e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104297" :question/problem "Differentiate ${e}^{x}cos⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=cos⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}cos⁡x)^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot cos⁡x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (cos⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(cos⁡x)+({e}^{x})(-sin⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}cos⁡x-{e}^{x}sin⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93eb24a9-f358-50cf-b447-c394864843f9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Combination of Functions Given the Necessary Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e705d0a-4f00-58c5-bfa3-0640c06a24cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238461} {:answer/id #uuid "950e015a-4dbb-56c7-9552-5f98ebc267f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239344} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d5826c-0581-5379-9a42-fe9bbf00aba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239915} {:answer/id #uuid "eceb79d2-6cd4-5cc9-bccd-243524ee9bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240138} {:answer/id #uuid "eeae5780-1ac7-5634-88fc-1ad4eb87c4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eceb79d2-6cd4-5cc9-bccd-243524ee9bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240138} :answer-field/id #uuid "32d5a9ca-fa2d-5f5e-8810-22ea172d70d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1d55ff1-19e4-58c5-a23a-1cef5eceb55b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104453" :question/problem "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=-6$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-6$ | $-2\\pi $ | $3\\pi $ | $5$ | $-2$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+2g(x))$ at $x=-6.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+2g(x))={f}^{′}(x)+2{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-6$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+2g(x))|}_{x=-6} & ={f}^{′}(-6)+2{g}^{′}(-6) \\\\  & =5+2(-2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "517f2054-008e-54eb-89dd-5f0a09e1f881" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Cubic Polynomial Given Three Real Roots and a Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "180ef558-9ed6-5cce-9dca-f397cbca2a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238260} {:answer/id #uuid "6abfd956-c66a-5d7e-9340-3d2575686da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238980} {:answer/id #uuid "80bf18c2-7c82-59a1-b1d0-128cc04043f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239173} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5e41f2-4fd2-5db5-81f2-fd469f282128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239553} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a37964-a889-594f-a3c0-5f08cd37e2c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6abfd956-c66a-5d7e-9340-3d2575686da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238980} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2da357a-9c04-5bb4-a19d-1edcb5991e71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b7607d8-f365-578e-840c-7619cca90194" :question/math-academy-id "q-104469" :question/problem "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-5,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=1,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $-2.$ Find $P(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=-5,$ ${x}_{1}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=1,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-2,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) & =-2(x-(-5))(x-0)(x-1) \\\\  & =-2x(x+5)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x(x+5)(x-1) & =-2x({x}^{2}+4x-5) \\\\  & =-2({x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-5x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+10x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059744 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cf627a0-6921-58ee-91c0-2f225d425711" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Transpose of a Row Vector or Column Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "833663bf-3970-5af6-8e3a-39f84ce0af74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056156} {:answer/id #uuid "848e9a7a-7c07-5311-acc3-3f32cc5e5be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056157} {:answer/id #uuid "88494fb1-c066-508f-b55b-d80ff6428bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 4 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056158} {:answer/id #uuid "ce89aba2-05bf-5b03-9f7b-8c7a1ac5d7ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056159} {:answer/id #uuid "06e646e6-1255-5a25-8e64-5d9c2c72a532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -5 & 4 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056160}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c3dafa3b-f8f7-511e-a394-16441e7131df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056155} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b40ab3-2e2d-5b11-8aee-c2a90901f88a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386090} {:answer/id #uuid "b462dd7b-c55c-5859-ab4a-5ce80868e897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & 4 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386091} {:answer/id #uuid "23804c66-ee95-50c0-84c0-7909c68a7143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386092} {:answer/id #uuid "c298e082-bb98-5541-8236-4d81c70f22ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386093} {:answer/id #uuid "343a923e-fc18-5408-a231-6bdb1ea178da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 4 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c298e082-bb98-5541-8236-4d81c70f22ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386093} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f1df5bc-2c6e-5f27-92f8-344c9f3c4bb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f200fb1-1ac4-518a-a135-3b50068ae3b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-1045" :question/problem "Find ${Z}^{T}$ given that \n$Z=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns. \n\nHere, there is just a single row, so it becomes a single column:\n\n$$\n{Z}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57187716-c00b-5428-a83a-18836d6b3d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Transpose of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86b41226-e408-58fa-979b-cc8b45e98ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386115} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5fd916-088a-5166-a538-72ebe72e4bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386116} {:answer/id #uuid "01bcfd1e-33a3-5426-bd02-020463692e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386117} {:answer/id #uuid "022a1834-fda3-5d40-bde2-1fd310284924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386118} {:answer/id #uuid "f86b76d5-d01e-510d-96f5-f8a51a52467b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f86b76d5-d01e-510d-96f5-f8a51a52467b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386119} :answer-field/id #uuid "6652d0ba-d453-5e9e-b621-000a54f9c592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63467001-d681-5463-af29-def2aaee49d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-1047" :question/problem "If\n    $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$,\nthen ${A}^{T}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42d6d200-1229-543a-a37b-cfbad294e448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238636} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2cb51c-ec33-5512-8fcf-cffc6022a24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238833} {:answer/id #uuid "5a33cd6b-6ff9-5210-b804-46da5ade64e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238834} {:answer/id #uuid "a93a95ab-5630-551b-a1d6-7702ae120fed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239537} {:answer/id #uuid "cb940dbd-f728-50f9-b403-fe9b67aba72d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb940dbd-f728-50f9-b403-fe9b67aba72d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239839} :answer-field/id #uuid "10300349-53f6-5f1f-a25c-0f8a6d2361f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5f39790-6626-50e4-936f-eda5c3ab471c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104784" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5bcb5723747beb905cc93e3bcf8e3a5be7bf566cf408998d3173490bd2486b5.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-3)$ where $h(x)=-3f(x)-g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=-3{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-3)=-3{f}^{′}(-3)-{g}^{′}(-3).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-3$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-5,-2)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-5,-1)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f8b5f4c3a87f6cb1c346a4672df96a068d74690f0b7c9c6f9a77a9a3710a34a.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-5,-2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-1-3}{-2-(-5)}=-\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-3)=-\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-5,-1)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{4-4}{-1-(-5)}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-3)=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-3) & =-3{f}^{′}(-3)-{g}^{′}(-3) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21735c55-5295-5f63-8b39-3e34d7671567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238345} {:answer/id #uuid "5dec431e-9a9f-5c0d-811c-6bb898eb228f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238873} {:answer/id #uuid "8522d0e6-bbbf-5d50-b7d3-88ed4d7d0e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239212} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fd6a95-3ea1-5f17-83b6-f1a30e2859ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239741} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e29836-e301-53f4-8965-c8033d7eecb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0fd6a95-3ea1-5f17-83b6-f1a30e2859ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239741} :answer-field/id #uuid "239b9480-5a2e-584d-b6a5-d6367605f4e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b935503-7332-565f-9b91-c43e7b0904d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-104786" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c4260d8cdf3557b6c6f762bf83862297812368d521104ba27995778a0cb0ff7.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)+2g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)+2{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)={f}^{′}(-2)+2{g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-2$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/4c/4ced2c562500bfa0d352d4552f38f47b5caab9bdc68db5bc3dbfca43799a0f8f.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{0-4}{0-(-4)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{2-4}{0-(-4)}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={f}^{′}(-2)+2{g}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =-1+2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e41277-3773-5e50-b48b-3e07ec725687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238104} {:answer/id #uuid "1a00f49c-5c8e-5e22-91ce-65f418c55c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238271} {:answer/id #uuid "24144e35-1ecf-550b-9f0d-0e56bd4dd66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238373} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9a5995-a974-5e53-80dd-de5ec2c79e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239121} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ad6ad5-505a-576a-9124-401f591be2c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24144e35-1ecf-550b-9f0d-0e56bd4dd66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238373} :answer-field/id #uuid "88a678ca-db36-56d8-badf-56a0bf3dbb10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d460086c-0f96-558c-85a2-2debf9c57ea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104787" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e02f873d2d583767e0b29e7ad57121e23e5c73716ce058ef6bed09b644b37bce.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=2f(x)+g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=2{f}^{′}(x)+{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=2{f}^{′}(-1)+{g}^{′}(-1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-1$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-2,1)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f64bb3d174950f3c4ac26d7d65d0b3b7881761fa3fb2ddd166f3a23c4af7fa27.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{0-4}{0-(-4)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-2,1)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{2-4}{1-(-2)}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-1)=-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =2{f}^{′}(-1)+{g}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35622553-ec3a-571d-9154-58bd121230ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238518} {:answer/id #uuid "b83b00bc-0ac3-5b8b-a8cc-3b01d437f685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239659} {:answer/id #uuid "cb549930-56b5-58ab-afec-ab7717bfe31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239836} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c96ede-23e9-5d02-8dec-ef47a8f02f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240061} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f90799-a3d9-5adb-a28f-7b8ae265ae83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb549930-56b5-58ab-afec-ab7717bfe31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239836} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dbca923-c686-5122-b696-d5906f3d500f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "720b1d63-4a80-5bc1-95a2-9593bdc22709" :question/math-academy-id "q-104803" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec9faab92da8de318d78e548adc35e8df20083816f8bafdacf0cfbbd497e084c.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The product rule and the addition rule for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=0$ and $g(-2)=3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)-{f}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 3+0\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c249cf3a-7864-5132-80fa-c18a3ea915c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercepts of a Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f34356-f0fd-54ea-96c4-4957c9922388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238531} {:answer/id #uuid "819b51de-dbb5-5a89-9680-f562b52c1ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239181} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7e1557-c374-5e2b-b72d-bc995c6d5497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239572} {:answer/id #uuid "d19b865d-acb8-54da-8e9d-d5708ac819aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239893} {:answer/id #uuid "f69f6643-1f61-5ff7-9a7b-1a9defeadcb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d19b865d-acb8-54da-8e9d-d5708ac819aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239893} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2a9c933-299f-5428-8219-f664a9ed4307" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be689818-78df-5d0a-8d4e-d472b73371da" :question/math-academy-id "q-10484" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $25{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2}=225.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2} & =225 \\\\ 25{y}^{2}-9(0)^{2} & =225 \\\\ 25{y}^{2} & =225 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ y & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,\\pm 3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Closed Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b055651-f48a-530d-8839-a9fa6a507120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d5cfd29b266bee94ed812edb71ac9804e385c9523341816be98ac278c088ef3b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386143} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f9476b-4797-542f-9fde-051e67ff10dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f0f65307224a5fea45530fd542be70d91039bc55aa2cd8b9f17537874c5170ae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386144} {:answer/id #uuid "da2a0717-9579-538a-8bd6-b64145d8464f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5b/5b2ca56f8c5c41015e6b0dc42311892becbb9f99233f5f9ddee951b993af95d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386145} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e135a4-1181-5606-acd5-846a34eda456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f1/f13573a5bfe5661e1b9853ea559c88f48a887b3fa0618605b7f3a4c933645af3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386146} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6cfa89-3814-51ff-86b5-d443219fa5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/03/03d8fa6c421deebb49b3746425f799a2eea7fc22f07175bb1cf80d270f896555.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e6cfa89-3814-51ff-86b5-d443219fa5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/03/03d8fa6c421deebb49b3746425f799a2eea7fc22f07175bb1cf80d270f896555.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386147} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d6e79ef-5309-5809-b54b-e5b877f96e8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f10cb2f1-287e-54d9-a68e-50d79d0f8000" :question/math-academy-id "q-105181" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[1,4]?$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](images/03/03d8fa6c421deebb49b3746425f799a2eea7fc22f07175bb1cf80d270f896555.png)\n\n  \n\nIndeed, this function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty ,1)$ and $(1,5).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=1.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $[1,5).$\n\nSo, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,1)$ and $[1,5).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nTherefore, this function is continuous on $[1,4]$ because $[1,4]$ is contained inside the interval $[1,5).$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $q(x)$ is not right-continuous at $x=1,$ so it is not continuous on the interval $[1,4].$\n\n- $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=2,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[1,4].$\n\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=3.$ So it is not continuous on the interval $[1,4].$\n\n- $p(x)$ is not defined at $x=4,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[1,4].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbb56f0a-ead0-5794-9e33-7fe0e1f87dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00535c78-693d-578f-9854-35ede0f5ea84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238081} {:answer/id #uuid "56c48d9d-1d29-5486-a7a5-24c09b6716ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238804} {:answer/id #uuid "9df9ae80-6d01-5879-b89f-4b4c816fb271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239436} {:answer/id #uuid "a2edb5df-f3ab-5a56-b218-c1793bbbc5b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239473} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e52b5d-7e6c-50b9-8aa1-34d4c2e3f3a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1e52b5d-7e6c-50b9-8aa1-34d4c2e3f3a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239899} :answer-field/id #uuid "e98e4466-7268-5d92-b403-0d0b4b3f5a67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "454b86d7-c6a0-5ac5-a0b5-59bf704396c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-105298" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ if $f(x)=ln⁡x-5csc⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x-5csc⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-5(-csc⁡xcot⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+5csc⁡xcot⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}+5csc⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})cot⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }+5\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4b3974-1288-524e-a34c-8091c0e072f1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "286fa88f-8ea5-503e-8fb0-428138ec40ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{x}sec⁡x(1+tan⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238410} {:answer/id #uuid "332c8633-3843-5c31-a194-e34735ef2602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{x}sec⁡x(1-tan⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238499} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc2bfad-0679-520a-922e-89fcbb185bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{x}sec⁡x(ln⁡5+tan⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239269} {:answer/id #uuid "9e62b7b8-8b3e-5c27-ac36-76b46c3019f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{x}sec⁡x(ln⁡5-tan⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239440} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d4930a-e248-568f-8ae5-64a2d0673eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{x}sec⁡xtan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bc2bfad-0679-520a-922e-89fcbb185bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{x}sec⁡x(ln⁡5+tan⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239269} :answer-field/id #uuid "f62f2c07-95e1-52c0-8500-ebb6139248e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd297ed3-dba2-5110-9b3e-0d62167228f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-105314" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y={5}^{x}sec⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{x}sec⁡x) \\\\  & ={5}^{x}ln⁡5sec⁡x+{5}^{x}sec⁡xtan⁡x \\\\  & ={5}^{x}sec⁡x(ln⁡5+tan⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078486 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbfddefc-d68b-5b1e-9ec5-fd18be204d92" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Semi-Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95be621a-8e1d-55d0-bedd-886439401e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/24/24551c0a0d905070625c0d1ef76fa942dab6764c1540f7ed317eda533dca06d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386150} {:answer/id #uuid "c9dd3ecc-32ea-517a-80ba-77de5c86fe90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/99/99e22a7485ab6e5b2a086418678d90160644814965aac3a3d1aede04e7cc6f2c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386151} {:answer/id #uuid "04869119-329b-5c6a-a260-8d54ed401acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6f54ad54f5b89f674469c29781d416f0d498855c6d788104d47a8119fa197e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386152} {:answer/id #uuid "47a534a2-65c8-5bd1-bf59-bed04c129751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7a/7afb04e0197b44ffadeee53bc365910f7c578eb8a3f2e2971a83f4f51947a3af.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386153} {:answer/id #uuid "65f7a984-1ea5-572a-962d-aa8344cc2b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/95587cfce50fd6e9c448bf3485dd98f7dd5bb401778b7a9fac939d356a112677.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47a534a2-65c8-5bd1-bf59-bed04c129751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7a/7afb04e0197b44ffadeee53bc365910f7c578eb8a3f2e2971a83f4f51947a3af.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386153} :answer-field/id #uuid "712fe9c6-4384-5603-a640-00082f69502a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19bb2540-93a4-5094-9d6e-6f7514d17932" :question/math-academy-id "q-105413" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,2]?$  You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](images/7a/7afb04e0197b44ffadeee53bc365910f7c578eb8a3f2e2971a83f4f51947a3af.png)\n\nWe see that $q(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,3],$ and since $(-\\infty ,2]$ lies completely inside $(-\\infty ,3],$ it is also continuous on $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are not continuous at $x=-1$, and so are not continuous on $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\n- $p(x)$ and $h(x)$ are not continuous at $x=0$, and so are not continuous on $(-\\infty ,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "195aa6d7-fbb5-5b8f-8c0f-0df7a844ad59" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Radical Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "338f00e3-a9b6-5b01-bddf-a5c466df3f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-6,-4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238502} {:answer/id #uuid "599e0cf6-447e-5db7-8508-e4f02a003d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238825} {:answer/id #uuid "a60f1e40-6ae7-5e2a-8e0b-639fdc076d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239495} {:answer/id #uuid "a69c5142-b462-592b-9d7d-056927fd4cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239499} {:answer/id #uuid "adfc3ccb-a635-5ff7-a12f-5807ec47070e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adfc3ccb-a635-5ff7-a12f-5807ec47070e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239565} :answer-field/id #uuid "702cd0a4-66af-5a7f-b88f-638c19246f33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca37f7e8-181f-55c3-97d4-d508fce578da" :question/math-academy-id "q-105436" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{4+x}+\\sqrt{3x+18}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "Our function consists of two radical functions, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{4+x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+x\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{3x+18}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+18\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,3x\\ge -18\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is given by the intersection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n[-4,\\infty )\\cap [-6,\\infty )=[-4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eac988ba-ea97-52c7-90a7-d451ee7f49e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Rational Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "061b9cef-b395-5e39-8736-5c43cd7560e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,8)\\cup (8,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238130} {:answer/id #uuid "227a783f-b222-5968-8336-3a05879831e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238358} {:answer/id #uuid "937612a4-df7c-557f-a151-80cf0fcc904e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-8)\\cup (-8,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239328} {:answer/id #uuid "f05b547a-3f39-5928-b515-f662fc295acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240175} {:answer/id #uuid "fabfffe4-3685-5469-9962-17b3e11df863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "937612a4-df7c-557f-a151-80cf0fcc904e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-8)\\cup (-8,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239328} :answer-field/id #uuid "270933c9-9ff7-561b-884e-ccb847b9d347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "977ff0f6-918f-5e62-ab1b-629afb73b4b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105465" :question/problem "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{2x+16}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{2x+16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{2(x+8)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=-8.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,-8)$ and $(-8,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-8)\\cup (-8,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1d07a6d-3994-5620-a756-ee6e2c0a8de6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of the Product or Quotient of a Composition of Functions Given the Necessary Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36608c7a-b754-5c40-a096-aa30e2178b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238524} {:answer/id #uuid "3da5dba9-c251-53dc-84d9-f74c59388074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238593} {:answer/id #uuid "6427110b-45b1-52cd-99de-02daa678454f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238932} {:answer/id #uuid "8ceeafb2-c08e-5bec-89b4-30b376d60877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239279} {:answer/id #uuid "dc33d4c5-aa2e-57eb-953e-22ee1afbe92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3da5dba9-c251-53dc-84d9-f74c59388074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238593} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc270617-7a3c-5b43-a25a-43255c8e50f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07342c4b-800a-56e7-9f4a-94fbd8dcbb7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105495" :question/problem "For the functions $p(x)$ and $q(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\np(10)=-1,\\,{p}^{′}(10)=-2,\\,q(10)=2,\\,{q}^{′}(10)=1\n$$\n\nIf $z(x)=(2p(x)+q(x))(2-p(x))$, compute ${z}^{′}(10).$" :question/worked-solution "The product rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(uv)=u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2p(x)+q(x)$ and $v=2-p(x).$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{p}^{′}(x)+{q}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}(x) & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(2p(x)+q(x))(-{p}^{′}(x))+(2-p(x))(2{p}^{′}(x)+{q}^{′}(x)) \\\\  & =-{p}^{′}(x)(2p(x)+q(x))+(2{p}^{′}(x)+{q}^{′}(x))(2-p(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=10,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}(10) & =-{p}^{′}(10)(2p(10)+q(10))+(2{p}^{′}(10)+{q}^{′}(10))(2-p(10)) \\\\  & =-(-2)(2(-1)+2)+(2(-2)+1)(2-(-1)) \\\\  & =2(-2+2)-3(2+1) \\\\  & =0-9 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054650 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95627f8c-4151-5565-8803-7558bd674acb" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Non-Square Matrices"}] :